{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Prototype code is disposable and its only job is to answer a question quickly, so judging it by behavior is usually the fastest path to learning. Keep it isolated from real users, data, credentials, and production infrastructure so unread code cannot create lasting risk."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo needs to be credible and repeatable, so review the paths stakeholders will see and test the core flows rather than reading every line. Treat the code as disposable and do not let it become the production foundation without an explicit hardening pass."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "At this stage speed still matters, but real users mean failures have consequences and the code will start to accrue changes. Use selective review plus automated tests, and raise authentication, payments, personal data, and other high-risk paths to level 2 or higher."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and an ongoing maintenance burden make unread changes and selective review too risky as a default. Require line-by-line review of AI-generated changes, supported by tests and observability, and relax it only for genuinely low-risk, well-tested areas with strong compensating controls."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, accountability, traceability, and correctness cannot be delegated to an unreviewed model. AI may suggest code, but an accountable engineer should write or fully rewrite and verify every line, backed by independent review and the applicable standards; use level 0 where the rules require it."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Use AI most aggressively when code is disposable and least aggressively as real users, money, safety, or regulatory obligations enter the picture. A practical default ladder is 4 for throwaway prototypes, 3 for demos and early MVPs, 2 for production, and 1 for critical systems, with risk-based escalation for sensitive code and an explicit rule that code cannot graduate to a higher stage without the corresponding review and testing."
}

